"<strong>Radii from the contacts.</strong> An eclipse has four contacts: first (the disks touch), second (the smaller disk is entirely in front of or behind the larger), third and fourth. For a central eclipse the smaller star crosses in a nearly straight line at the speed \\(v\\), so",
"\\[R_{\\text{large}} + R_{\\text{small}} = \\tfrac12 v\\,(t_4 - t_1),\\qquad R_{\\text{large}} - R_{\\text{small}} = \\tfrac12 v\\,(t_3 - t_2).\\]",
"<strong>Light and temperature.</strong> A star’s luminosity is \\(L = 4\\pi R^2\\sigma T^4\\). When the smaller star is completely hidden (a total eclipse), the brightness left is the larger star’s share of the light, \\(f = L_1/(L_1 + L_2)\\). When equal areas are hidden, the eclipse depths are in the ratio of the surface brightnesses, \\(T^4\\).",
